message.content:
Below is a **complete, self‑contained recipe** for taking a slice out of a raw `char[]` (`textBuffer`) – starting at position 5, grabbing ten characters – escaping those characters for safe inclusion inside an XML document, and finally streaming them into whatever destination you’re working with (a file, network socket, servlet response …).  
I’ll cover both “plain” I/O and the more modern StAX API because most people either still use `java.io.*` streams or prefer the lightweight `XMLStreamWriter`.

---

## 1️⃣ What you actually need

| Step | Why |
|------|-----|
| **Extract the slice** | You only want part of the buffer. |
| **Escape XML specials** | `<`, `>`, `"`, `'`, `&` would break your markup otherwise. |
| **Write to a *stream*** | Either a plain `Writer` or an `XMLStreamWriter`. |

### Quick sanity checks

```java
if (textBuffer == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("buffer cannot be null");
int start   = 5;
int length  = 10;

// Bounds guard – prevents IndexOutOfBoundsException
if (start < 0 || length < 0 ||
    start + length > textBuffer.length) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException(
        "Requested slice ("+start+", "+length+") exceeds buffer size "
        +textBuffer.length);
}
```

---

## 2️⃣ Plain `Writer` path (e.g., FileWriter, PrintWriter)

```java
import java.io.*;
import org.apache.commons.text.StringEscapeUtils;   // Maven: commons-text

public static void appendSliceToWriter(char[] textBuffer,
                                       int start, int length,
                                       Writer target) throws IOException {

    /* 1️⃣ Grab the slice */
    String slice = new String(textBuffer, start, length);      // cheap copy

    /* 2️⃣ Escape for XML */
    String escaped = StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml11(slice);     // uses XML 1.1 rules

    /* 3️⃣ Append to the writer */
    target.append(escaped);          // .write(...) works too
}
```

#### Usage example

```java
try (FileWriter fw = new FileWriter("/tmp/out.txt")) {       // auto‑flush close
    char[] buf = {'<', '&', '"', '\'', '>', ' ', 'H','i',
                  ',', ' ','w','o','r','l','d'};
    appendSliceToWriter(buf, 5, 10, fw);                      // writes " Hi,worl"
}
```

*Why `escapeXml11()` instead of `escapeXml13()`?*  
Both work fine for ordinary data; XML 1.1 allows a few extra control codes. If you know you’re targeting XML 1.0, swap to `escapeXml13()`—it’s essentially identical except for one edge case.

---

## 3️⃣ Using StAX (`XMLStreamWriter`) – best practice when building real XML documents

StAX gives you automatic character escaping *for free*, so you don’t even need the explicit `escapeXml…()` step unless you’re doing custom processing.

```java
import javax.xml.stream.*;

public static void appendSliceWithStax(char[] textBuffer,
                                      int start, int length,
                                      XMLStreamWriter xmlWriter) throws XMLStreamException {

    // Directly feed the slice – StAX escapes internally
    xmlWriter.writeCharacters(textBuffer, start, length);
}
```

#### Full demo

```java
// Create a simple XML skeleton first
XMLOutputFactory factory = XMLOutputFactory.newInstance();
ByteArrayOutputStream baos = new ByteArrayOutputStream();

XMLStreamWriter xsw = factory.createXMLStreamWriter(new OutputStreamWriter(baos, StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
xsw.writeStartDocument();            // <?xml version="1.0"?>
xsw.writeStartElement("root");       // <root>
xsw.writeAttribute("lang","en");

char[] buf = "<>&\"'Hello World!".toCharArray();

// Insert the slice safely
appendSliceWithStax(buf, 5, 10, xsw);   // inserts "Hello W"

xsw.writeEndElement();                // </root>
xsw.writeEndDocument();
xsw.flush();
xsw.close();

System.out.println(baos.toString(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));   // prints full XML
```

Result:

```xml
<?xml version="1.0"?><root lang="en">Hello W</root>
```

Notice there was **no manual escaping needed** – `writeCharacters(..)` handled all `<`, `>` etc automatically.

---

## 4️⃣ Handling *temporary vs. final* output state

Sometimes you may be buffering data temporarily (in memory) before committing it to disk/network. A common pattern is:

```java
StringBuilder temp = new StringBuilder();
// build up content …
temp.append("<partial>").append(someData).append("</partial>");

if (isFinalState()) {
    try (BufferedWriter bw = Files.newBufferedWriter(Paths.get(finalPath), StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {
        bw.write(temp.toString());
    }
} else {
    // keep temp around, log, retry later…
}
```

When switching between temporary and final stages, remember:

| State | Typical action |
|-------|----------------|
| Temporary | Keep in RAM / cache; optionally compress or encrypt |
| Final | Persist atomically (use `Files.move(..., ATOMIC_MOVE)` where supported) |

---

## 5️⃣ Common gotchas & tips

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| **IndexOutOfBound** | Validate `start + length <= buffer.length` beforehand. |
| **NullPointer** | Guard against `null` buffers or writers. |
| **Encoding mismatch** | Always specify UTF‑8 explicitly (`StandardCharsets.UTF_8`). |
| **Large slices** | For huge arrays avoid copying to `String`; let StAX consume the slice directly (`writeCharacters(ch,start,len)`). |
| **Thread safety** | Writers aren’t thread‑safe; synchronize externally or use separate instances per thread. |

---

## 6️⃣ TL;DR Code Snippet

```java
void appendSafeSubstring(char[] src, int off, int len, Writer dst) throws IOException {
    if (src==null||dst==null) throw new NullPointerException();
    if(off<0||len<0||off+len>src.length) throw new IllegalArgumentException();

    // Option 1: Manual escape (works everywhere)
    String esc = org.apache.commons.text.StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml11(src, off, len);
    dst.append(esc);

    // OR Option 2: Let StAX do it
    // XMLStreamWriter w = ...
    // w.writeCharacters(src, off, len);
}
```

Pick whichever route fits your stack. Happy coding!
